hey everybody welcome to reality check  now as we all know video games  frequently come under a lot of flack and  mainstream media for a variety of  reasons be it concerns over violent  content worries about their addictive  nature or gates anyway this episode is  not about any of that in fact I think  it's high time for some good news  positive video games stories so come  with me on this week's reality check as  we explore the latest cutting-edge  scientific research demonstrating that  playing video games can actually be good  for you  wonderful  do you remember when you learn to type  on a keyboard or perhaps to ride a bike  well a friend of mine who will remain  nameless actually managed to fail to  learn to ride a bike  twice pretty special huh  well you know what might have actually  helped her is playing more action video  games  you see tasks like typing and bike  riding which involve learning a new  pattern or coordination between your  vision and motor functions like moving  your arms and legs require what's called  sensory motor skills now a study led by  University of Toronto psychology  researchers found the people who play  action video games seem to be able to  learn new sensory motor skills more  quickly than non-gamers the experiment  carried out by the team involved two  groups one made up of 18 gamers who  regularly played first-person shooter  games and the other 18 non gamers who  had played little-to-no video games in  the past two years now to test  everyone's sensory motor skills they  didn't just chuck them all on bikes  because most normal people already know  how to do that no instead they had  everyone performed something called a  manual tracking task basically this  involved trying to keep a small green  square cursor at the center of a white  square moving target which dashed about  in a complicated but repeating pattern  now at the beginning there was pretty  much no difference between the gamers  and the non gamers they both picked up  the task initially at about the same  rate however by the close of the  experiment while everyone had improved  with time the gamers remarkably better  by the end according to the researchers  this is likely due to the gamers  superior ability in learning a novel  sensory motor pattern that is their  gaming experience enabled them to learn  better than the non gamers pretty  interesting stuff however the research  team weren't done just yet they had to  be sure that this improvement in the  gamers was down to learning the exact  task not just say because they were  better at controlling the mouse for  example so they repeated the experiment  but this time the pattern of movement of  the white target square was random and  didn't repeat this removed the  possibility of anyone learning the route  and lo and behold this time around  there was no difference between the  gamers and the non gamers  now when I was a little kid I played a  lot of Civilization with my dad and big  brother as well as being a hell of a lot  of fun this also gave me impeccable  knowledge of ancient capital cities that  an Sun Tzu's Art of War  well it turns out it's not just fact  learning the videogames can impart on  children in fact a recent study is  thrown up compelling evidence that young  people who play games can actually get a  boost in their overall English language  skills the study carried out to the  University of Gothenburg involved 76  young people aged 10 and 11 and  demonstrated that young gamers really  gain an advantage in terms of their  English vocabulary over the non gaming  contemporaries the key it seems to a  massing of voluminous lexicon is  elementally a consequence of periodic  exposure to novel prose in other words  it's not a function of the gameplay as  such it's all about being exposed to  more written and spoken English outside  of school and games are a very effective  way to do this massively multiplayer  online role-playing games  we're actually called out as being  particularly effective due to the large  numbers of real people all interacting  with one another in a virtual world as  associate professor lists Kirsten salvan  explained as a player you simply have to  be able to understand what's being said  to read English and to interact yourself  by both writing and speaking English  at the beginning of this episode we saw  some of the merits of playing action  titles but now it's a turn of puzzle  games these have been shown to improve  our overall mental flexibility and also  executive functions so what does that  mean  well it's basically our ability to think  on our feet to quickly problem-solve and  deal with sudden changes in the  environment the game used in the study  was none other than super popular mobile  puzzler cut the rope and the research  demonstrated that it took as little as  an hour a day of gaming to get the  improvements so how exactly did the  study work well a group of non-gamers  played for an hour a day five days a  week for four weeks the participants  were split into four groups each one  playing in different game once the four  weeks of gaming were over all test  subjects then took part in a number of  tests the results showed that players  have cut the rope could switch between  tasks 33% faster were 30% faster in  adapting to new situations and 60%  better in blocking out distractions and  focusing on the tasks at hand than  before training so how could games like  Cut the Rope actually help with this  well it's possibly to do with the fact  that games like Cut the Rope constantly  require you to employ new strategies in  order to progress a tactics and tricks  that you learn at the start probably  won't help you later on so you need to  be able to think creatively  according to assistant professor Michael  D Patterson this finding is important  because previously no video games have  demonstrated this type of broad  improvement to executive functions which  are important for general intelligence  dealing with these situations and  managing multitasking so what do you  make of this week's episode have you  ever observed any of these effects in  real life and can you think of any other  ways that video games might actually be  good for us let me know in the comments  down below or you can get in touch with  me directly over twitter at cam for  Ezreal and remember that's the place if  you want to suggest to me any potential  topics for reality  okay thanks for watching guys see you  next week
 

